  {"id":"starFeelingRealMiddlemarch2013","abstract":"This article enters the discussion of nineteenth-century realism and ontology by considering the phenomenology of reality-perception in George Eliot’s Middlemarch . As I show, Eliot’s formal innovations display a persistent concern, allied with contemporary studies of perception, with visual limitation as the defining quality of character reality. Eliot, I argue, creates a distinctly phenomenal realism by suggesting aesthetic reality to be something we designate and engage with through phenomenal as opposed to access consciousness. In this way, I also develop a new account of realist ethics: figuring limitation as the condition of both reality-perception and agency in the realization of self as contingent part.","accessed":{"date-parts":[["2025",9,10]]},"author":[{"family":"Star","given":"Summer J."}],"citation-key":"starFeelingRealMiddlemarch2013","container-title":"ELH","container-title-short":"elh","DOI":"10.1353/elh.2013.0030","ISSN":"1080-6547","issue":"3","issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",9]]},"language":"en","page":"839-869","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Feeling Real in Middlemarch","type":"article-journal","URL":"https://muse.jhu.edu/article/520495","volume":"80"},
